    user_response: "You are a Senior Product Designer, UX Architect, Cybersecurity SOC UX Specialist, and Frontend Product Architect.\n\nWe are building:\n\nASHIELDER\nAI-Powered Email Threat Detection, GeoLocation & Forensic Intelligence Platform\n\nSIH 2026\nPS 26106\n\nThe backend already exists and is being developed separately.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo NOT design or invent backend functionality.\n\nYour job is to research and define the CLIENT-SIDE PRODUCT EXPERIENCE.\n\nThe frontend will be a professional cybersecurity investigation workstation for:\n\n- SOC Analysts\n- Cybersecurity Analysts\n- Digital Forensic Investigators\n- Government / Enterprise Security Teams\n- Incident Response Teams\n\nThe UI must feel like:\n\nProfessional SOC platform\n+\nDigital Forensics workstation\n+\nModern enterprise dashboard\n+\nAI copilot\n\nIt must NOT look like:\n\n- ChatGPT clone\n- generic SaaS dashboard\n- gaming UI\n- hacker/cyberpunk interface\n- overly futuristic interface\n\n==================================================\nOBJECTIVE\n==================================================\n\nResearch and define the complete ASHIELDER client experience.\n\nFocus on:\n\n1. Information Architecture\n2. Navigation\n3. Case Management\n4. Investigation Workspace\n5. Forensic Report\n6. Evidence Explorer\n7. Investigation Journey\n8. Header Forensics\n9. URL Analysis\n10. Infrastructure / IP Intelligence\n11. Geolocation\n12. Attachments\n13. Historical Intelligence\n14. Re-investigation\n15. AI Assistant\n16. Risk / Confidence / Verdict\n17. Error States\n18. Loading States\n19. INCONCLUSIVE State\n20. RBAC-ready UX\n21. Responsive strategy\n22. Accessibility\n\n==================================================\nDESIGN PRINCIPLES\n==================================================\n\nEvidence First.\n\nSummary Before Detail.\n\nCurrent Case \342\211\240 Historical Context.\n\nSystem Finding \342\211\240 AI Explanation.\n\nRisk \342\211\240 Confidence.\n\nMissing Evidence \342\211\240 Safe.\n\nAI is Assistant, not Authority.\n\nInvestigation Journey must be explainable.\n\nForensic evidence must be traceable.\n\nThe analyst must always be able to inspect the source evidence behind an explanation.\n\n==================================================\nMAIN APPLICATION\n==================================================\n\nDesign these primary areas:\n\nAuthentication\n\nCase Inbox\n\nCase Workspace\n\nInvestigation\n\nEvidence Explorer\n\nHistory\n\nReports\n\nAssistant\n\nSettings / Administration\n\n==================================================\nCASE WORKSPACE\n==================================================\n\nDesign the main forensic workspace.\n\nRequired information:\n\nCase ID\nVerdict\nRisk\nConfidence\nSender\nSubject\nReceived Time\nInvestigation Status\n\nKey Findings\n\nInvestigation Journey\n\nEvidence\n\nHistorical Context\n\nInfrastructure\n\nAttachments\n\nAI Assistant\n\nActions:\n\nView Report\nAsk Assistant\nRe-investigate\nEscalate\n\n==================================================\nREPORT EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nDesign a professional forensic report.\n\nInclude:\n\nExecutive Summary\n\nVerdict\n\nRisk\n\nConfidence\n\nKey Findings\n\nAuthentication Analysis\n\nHeader Analysis\n\nReceived Chain\n\nURLs\n\nDomains\n\nIP Intelligence\n\nGeolocation\n\nAttachments\n\nHistorical Context\n\nInvestigation Journey\n\nMissing Evidence\n\nFailed Tools\n\nAudit / Integrity\n\nAI Explanation\n\nMake the difference between:\n\nSYSTEM FINDING\n\nand\n\nAI EXPLANATION\n\nobvious.\n\n==================================================\nAI ASSISTANT\n==================================================\n\nDesign an analyst assistant.\n\nExamples:\n\nWhy is this email suspicious?\n\nWhich evidence is strongest?\n\nWhat evidence is missing?\n\nWhy is confidence low?\n\nWhat should I investigate next?\n\nWhat historical cases are related?\n\nThe assistant must cite evidence IDs.\n\nIt must never visually appear to control:\n\nRisk\nConfidence\nVerdict\n\n==================================================\nVISUAL SYSTEM\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nTypography\nSpacing\nGrid\nCards\nTables\nBadges\nStatus styles\nIcons\nButtons\nTabs\nTimeline\nMap\nEvidence chips\nRisk visualization\nConfidence visualization\n\nUse a professional, sober cybersecurity aesthetic.\n\nAvoid excessive gradients and neon colors.\n\n==================================================\nLOW COGNITIVE LOAD\n==================================================\n\nThe analyst should understand a case within seconds.\n\nPrioritize:\n\nWhat happened?\n\nHow dangerous is it?\n\nWhy?\n\nWhat evidence supports it?\n\nWhat is missing?\n\nWhat should I do next?\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md\n\nUse:\n\n# Executive Summary\n\n# User Personas\n\n# Design Principles\n\n# Information Architecture\n\n# Navigation\n\n# Case Inbox\n\n# Case Workspace\n\n# Investigation View\n\n# Evidence Explorer\n\n# Header Forensics\n\n# URL Analysis\n\n# Infrastructure Intelligence\n\n# Geolocation\n\n# Attachments\n\n# Historical Intelligence\n\n# Re-investigation\n\n# Forensic Report\n\n# AI Assistant\n\n# Error States\n\n# Loading States\n\n# Empty States\n\n# INCONCLUSIVE State\n\n# Authentication UX\n\n# RBAC-ready UX\n\n# Accessibility\n\n# Responsive Strategy\n\n# Design System\n\n# Component Inventory\n\n# Page Inventory\n\n# User Journeys\n\n# MVP Scope\n\n# Future Scope\n\n# UX Risks\n\n# Final Recommendations\n\nDo NOT write code.\n\nDo NOT invent backend APIs.\n\nThis document will be used as the authoritative frontend product specification."
    items {
      text: "You are a Senior Product Designer, UX Architect, Cybersecurity SOC UX Specialist, and Frontend Product Architect.\n\nWe are building:\n\nASHIELDER\nAI-Powered Email Threat Detection, GeoLocation & Forensic Intelligence Platform\n\nSIH 2026\nPS 26106\n\nThe backend already exists and is being developed separately.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo NOT design or invent backend functionality.\n\nYour job is to research and define the CLIENT-SIDE PRODUCT EXPERIENCE.\n\nThe frontend will be a professional cybersecurity investigation workstation for:\n\n- SOC Analysts\n- Cybersecurity Analysts\n- Digital Forensic Investigators\n- Government / Enterprise Security Teams\n- Incident Response Teams\n\nThe UI must feel like:\n\nProfessional SOC platform\n+\nDigital Forensics workstation\n+\nModern enterprise dashboard\n+\nAI copilot\n\nIt must NOT look like:\n\n- ChatGPT clone\n- generic SaaS dashboard\n- gaming UI\n- hacker/cyberpunk interface\n- overly futuristic interface\n\n==================================================\nOBJECTIVE\n==================================================\n\nResearch and define the complete ASHIELDER client experience.\n\nFocus on:\n\n1. Information Architecture\n2. Navigation\n3. Case Management\n4. Investigation Workspace\n5. Forensic Report\n6. Evidence Explorer\n7. Investigation Journey\n8. Header Forensics\n9. URL Analysis\n10. Infrastructure / IP Intelligence\n11. Geolocation\n12. Attachments\n13. Historical Intelligence\n14. Re-investigation\n15. AI Assistant\n16. Risk / Confidence / Verdict\n17. Error States\n18. Loading States\n19. INCONCLUSIVE State\n20. RBAC-ready UX\n21. Responsive strategy\n22. Accessibility\n\n==================================================\nDESIGN PRINCIPLES\n==================================================\n\nEvidence First.\n\nSummary Before Detail.\n\nCurrent Case \342\211\240 Historical Context.\n\nSystem Finding \342\211\240 AI Explanation.\n\nRisk \342\211\240 Confidence.\n\nMissing Evidence \342\211\240 Safe.\n\nAI is Assistant, not Authority.\n\nInvestigation Journey must be explainable.\n\nForensic evidence must be traceable.\n\nThe analyst must always be able to inspect the source evidence behind an explanation.\n\n==================================================\nMAIN APPLICATION\n==================================================\n\nDesign these primary areas:\n\nAuthentication\n\nCase Inbox\n\nCase Workspace\n\nInvestigation\n\nEvidence Explorer\n\nHistory\n\nReports\n\nAssistant\n\nSettings / Administration\n\n==================================================\nCASE WORKSPACE\n==================================================\n\nDesign the main forensic workspace.\n\nRequired information:\n\nCase ID\nVerdict\nRisk\nConfidence\nSender\nSubject\nReceived Time\nInvestigation Status\n\nKey Findings\n\nInvestigation Journey\n\nEvidence\n\nHistorical Context\n\nInfrastructure\n\nAttachments\n\nAI Assistant\n\nActions:\n\nView Report\nAsk Assistant\nRe-investigate\nEscalate\n\n==================================================\nREPORT EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nDesign a professional forensic report.\n\nInclude:\n\nExecutive Summary\n\nVerdict\n\nRisk\n\nConfidence\n\nKey Findings\n\nAuthentication Analysis\n\nHeader Analysis\n\nReceived Chain\n\nURLs\n\nDomains\n\nIP Intelligence\n\nGeolocation\n\nAttachments\n\nHistorical Context\n\nInvestigation Journey\n\nMissing Evidence\n\nFailed Tools\n\nAudit / Integrity\n\nAI Explanation\n\nMake the difference between:\n\nSYSTEM FINDING\n\nand\n\nAI EXPLANATION\n\nobvious.\n\n==================================================\nAI ASSISTANT\n==================================================\n\nDesign an analyst assistant.\n\nExamples:\n\nWhy is this email suspicious?\n\nWhich evidence is strongest?\n\nWhat evidence is missing?\n\nWhy is confidence low?\n\nWhat should I investigate next?\n\nWhat historical cases are related?\n\nThe assistant must cite evidence IDs.\n\nIt must never visually appear to control:\n\nRisk\nConfidence\nVerdict\n\n==================================================\nVISUAL SYSTEM\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nTypography\nSpacing\nGrid\nCards\nTables\nBadges\nStatus styles\nIcons\nButtons\nTabs\nTimeline\nMap\nEvidence chips\nRisk visualization\nConfidence visualization\n\nUse a professional, sober cybersecurity aesthetic.\n\nAvoid excessive gradients and neon colors.\n\n==================================================\nLOW COGNITIVE LOAD\n==================================================\n\nThe analyst should understand a case within seconds.\n\nPrioritize:\n\nWhat happened?\n\nHow dangerous is it?\n\nWhy?\n\nWhat evidence supports it?\n\nWhat is missing?\n\nWhat should I do next?\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md\n\nUse:\n\n# Executive Summary\n\n# User Personas\n\n# Design Principles\n\n# Information Architecture\n\n# Navigation\n\n# Case Inbox\n\n# Case Workspace\n\n# Investigation View\n\n# Evidence Explorer\n\n# Header Forensics\n\n# URL Analysis\n\n# Infrastructure Intelligence\n\n# Geolocation\n\n# Attachments\n\n# Historical Intelligence\n\n# Re-investigation\n\n# Forensic Report\n\n# AI Assistant\n\n# Error States\n\n# Loading States\n\n# Empty States\n\n# INCONCLUSIVE State\n\n# Authentication UX\n\n# RBAC-ready UX\n\n# Accessibility\n\n# Responsive Strategy\n\n# Design System\n\n# Component Inventory\n\n# Page Inventory\n\n# User Journeys\n\n# MVP Scope\n\n# Future Scope\n\n# UX Risks\n\n# Final Recommendations\n\nDo NOT write code.\n\nDo NOT invent backend APIs.\n\nThis document will be used as the authoritative frontend product specification."
