  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Product Spec**\n\nI\'m now diving into the ASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md file. My focus is reviewing its section requirements to ensure a comprehensive product specification. Currently, I\'m focusing on the Executive Summary, User Personas, Design Principles, and Information Architecture sections, looking for a clear and concise framework. After that, I will review the Navigation, Case Inbox, Case Workspace, and Investigation View.\n\n\n**Exploring Feature Concepts**\n\nI\'ve been brainstorming potential feature concepts for the platform. Focusing on the \"Evidence Explorer\" and \"URL Analysis\" areas, alongside \"Infrastructure Intelligence\" and \"Geolocation.\" Furthermore, I will include attachment analysis, historical intelligence, and re-investigation capabilities. I\'m aiming for a suite of tools that meet cybersecurity and digital forensics needs.\n\n\n**Defining the UI/UX**\n\nI\'m now concretizing the user interface and user experience design. I\'m prioritizing a sober, high-density aesthetic with dark slate or refined cool neutrals. I am focusing on crisp typography and clear borders, with monospace evidence blocks. The design principles are formalized: Evidence First, Summary Before Detail, clear separation between current case and historical context, and distinguishing between system findings and AI explanations. The Case Workspace and Report Experience are being refined to reflect these principles.\n\n\n**Refining AI Integration**\n\nI\'m now expanding the AI Assistant\'s capabilities. It will analyze email headers (SPF, DKIM, DMARC, ARC, BIMI), received chains (latency, IP validation), URLs, domains, IPs, geolocations, and attachments, all within a historical context. The assistant should address missing evidence, failed tools, and audit/integrity issues. A crucial design aspect: *clearly differentiating SYSTEM FINDINGS from AI EXPLANATIONS*. The assistant will cite evidence IDs in its responses. The UI will maintain a professional aesthetic, emphasizing low cognitive load for quick case understanding.\n\n\n**Structuring the Specification**\n\nI\'m now outlining the comprehensive structure of `ASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md`. I am ensuring that all 34 required sections are present and detailed, starting with the Executive Summary, User Personas, and Architectural Stance. I intend to build a robust foundation for all 34 required sections. I am also planning the depth and rigor required for each section to provide an exhaustive, professional product design specification. I\'ll make sure the `App.tsx` file delivers an interactive, detailed spec workstation reader.\n\n\n**Refining User Personas**\n\nI\'m now refining the user personas. I\'ve broken them down into four specific roles: SOC Analyst L1 focusing on triage and false positive filtration, SOC Analyst L2/Incident Responder for root-cause and threat hunting, Digital Forensics Examiner/Legal-Admissibility Lead for RFC parsing and audit trails, and Government/CERT Lead/Enterprise CISO for strategic posture and IOC sharing. I\'m focusing on defining the explicit needs of each persona.\n\n\n\n**Defining the Application Architecture**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the application\'s architecture from the front-end perspective. I\'m clarifying the conceptual data model and defining the global hierarchy, starting with the system shell and going through the workspace, inspectors, drawers, and the audit modal. I am also working through the various state hierarchies like case, view, inspector, and copilot contexts.\n\n\n\n**Developing the Investigation View**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the specifics of the investigation view. I\'m fleshing out the timeline of heuristic and model executions and building the step-by-step diagnostic chain. Also, I\'m working through the dependency graph of detections and failure visualizers to enhance the investigation process. Finally, I\'m working on the quick action bar (view report, re-investigate, export) along with its metadata (e.g. Sender, Received Timestamp, Case Status)\n\n\n**Expanding Feature Analysis**\n\nI\'m now expanding the feature analysis to focus on Infrastructure Intelligence, Geolocation, and Attachments. I\'m focusing on features such as typosquatting visualization, reverse DNS timelines, and attachment analysis. I\'m prioritizing features like ASN/ISP reputation and reverse DNS history. Then, I\'ll dive into attachment analysis. I am designing a comprehensive suite of features.\n\n\n**Elaborating Feature Detail**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Historical Intelligence\" and \"Re-investigation\" features. I\'m focusing on the Similarity Engine UI, campaign correlation radar, and the visual separation of evidence. I\'m also addressing re-analysis triggers, diff modes for comparative inspection, and version history. Finally, I\'m defining a formal report structure, interactive in-app reports, and court-admissible PDFs with clear distinctions between system findings and AI explanations.\n\n\n**Designing Error States**\n\nI\'m now focusing on critical error states for the application. Specifically, I\'m working through Tool Execution Timeouts, partial data ingestion issues, and scenarios where feeds are unavailable or require offline intel fallback. I\'m also considering parsing failures with malformed RFC 5322 or corrupt EML files.\n\n\n\n**Prioritizing User Roles**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on Authentication, RBAC, and Accessibility features, designing them with a focus on user experience. I\'m building with Enterprise SSO, MFA, and forensic state recovery in mind. I\'m focusing on a role matrix that maps actions, dynamic UI with permission tooltips, and WCAG 2.1 AA compliance. I\'m designing responsive layouts for multi-monitor display walls, standard workstations, IR laptops, and even mobile field response, so that they\'re all user-friendly. I will then ensure the design system incorporates the appropriate color palette.\n\n\n**Defining the UI Kit**\n\nI\'m now detailing the application\'s UI kit, working through color palettes: Deep Obsidian, Charcoal Slate, Border Line, Severe/Critical Red, Suspicious Amber, Informational Blue, Safe Teal/Green, and Neutral Gray. I am also working through Typography Scales and Monospace selection, including JetBrains Mono/IBM Plex Mono for evidence and Inter/Plus Jakarta Sans for UI. The design will include spacing rhythms, radii, elevation, borders, badges, buttons, tabs, risk gauges, and confidence gauge specifications. I will then inventory components, pages, user journeys, and the MVP scope.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 86
      nanos: 651433102
    }
