    user_requests: "User prompt: You are a Senior Product Designer, UX Architect, Cybersecurity SOC UX Specialist, and Frontend Product Architect.\n\nWe are building:\n\nASHIELDER\nAI-Powered Email Threat Detection, GeoLocation & Forensic Intelligence Platform\n\nSIH 2026\nPS 26106\n\nThe backend already exists and is being developed separately.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo NOT design or invent backend functionality.\n\nYour job is to research and define the CLIENT-SIDE PRODUCT EXPERIENCE.\n\nThe frontend will be a professional cybersecurity investigation workstation for:\n\n- SOC Analysts\n- Cybersecurity Analysts\n- Digital Forensic Investigators\n- Government / Enterprise Security Teams\n- Incident Response Teams\n\nThe UI must feel like:\n\nProfessional SOC platform\n+\nDigital Forensics workstation\n+\nModern enterprise dashboard\n+\nAI copilot\n\nIt must NOT look like:\n\n- ChatGPT clone\n- generic SaaS dashboard\n- gaming UI\n- hacker/cyberpunk interface\n- overly futuristic interface\n\n==================================================\nOBJECTIVE\n==================================================\n\nResearch and define the complete ASHIELDER client experience.\n\nFocus on:\n\n1. Information Architecture\n2. Navigation\n3. Case Management\n4. Investigation Workspace\n5. Forensic Report\n6. Evidence Explorer\n7. Investigation Journey\n8. Header Forensics\n9. URL Analysis\n10. Infrastructure / IP Intelligence\n11. Geolocation\n12. Attachments\n13. Historical Intelligence\n14. Re-investigation\n15. AI Assistant\n16. Risk / Confidence / Verdict\n17. Error States\n18. Loading States\n19. INCONCLUSIVE State\n20. RBAC-ready UX\n21. Responsive strategy\n22. Accessibility\n\n==================================================\nDESIGN PRINCIPLES\n==================================================\n\nEvidence First.\n\nSummary Before Detail.\n\nCurrent Case \342\211\240 Historical Context.\n\nSystem Finding \342\211\240 AI Explanation.\n\nRisk \342\211\240 Confidence.\n\nMissing Evidence \342\211\240 Safe.\n\nAI is Assistant, not Authority.\n\nInvestigation Journey must be explainable.\n\nForensic evidence must be traceable.\n\nThe analyst must always be able to inspect the source evidence behind an explanation.\n\n==================================================\nMAIN APPLICATION\n==================================================\n\nDesign these primary areas:\n\nAuthentication\n\nCase Inbox\n\nCase Workspace\n\nInvestigation\n\nEvidence Explorer\n\nHistory\n\nReports\n\nAssistant\n\nSettings / Administration\n\n==================================================\nCASE WORKSPACE\n==================================================\n\nDesign the main forensic workspace.\n\nRequired information:\n\nCase ID\nVerdict\nRisk\nConfidence\nSender\nSubject\nReceived Time\nInvestigation Status\n\nKey Findings\n\nInvestigation Journey\n\nEvidence\n\nHistorical Context\n\nInfrastructure\n\nAttachments\n\nAI Assistant\n\nActions:\n\nView Report\nAsk Assistant\nRe-investigate\nEscalate\n\n==================================================\nREPORT EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nDesign a professional forensic report.\n\nInclude:\n\nExecutive Summary\n\nVerdict\n\nRisk\n\nConfidence\n\nKey Findings\n\nAuthentication Analysis\n\nHeader Analysis\n\nReceived Chain\n\nURLs\n\nDomains\n\nIP Intelligence\n\nGeolocation\n\nAttachments\n\nHistorical Context\n\nInvestigation Journey\n\nMissing Evidence\n\nFailed Tools\n\nAudit / Integrity\n\nAI Explanation\n\nMake the difference between:\n\nSYSTEM FINDING\n\nand\n\nAI EXPLANATION\n\nobvious.\n\n==================================================\nAI ASSISTANT\n==================================================\n\nDesign an analyst assistant.\n\nExamples:\n\nWhy is this email suspicious?\n\nWhich evidence is strongest?\n\nWhat evidence is missing?\n\nWhy is confidence low?\n\nWhat should I investigate next?\n\nWhat historical cases are related?\n\nThe assistant must cite evidence IDs.\n\nIt must never visually appear to control:\n\nRisk\nConfidence\nVerdict\n\n==================================================\nVISUAL SYSTEM\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nTypography\nSpacing\nGrid\nCards\nTables\nBadges\nStatus styles\nIcons\nButtons\nTabs\nTimeline\nMap\nEvidence chips\nRisk visualization\nConfidence visualization\n\nUse a professional, sober cybersecurity aesthetic.\n\nAvoid excessive gradients and neon colors.\n\n==================================================\nLOW COGNITIVE LOAD\n==================================================\n\nThe analyst should understand a case within seconds.\n\nPrioritize:\n\nWhat happened?\n\nHow dangerous is it?\n\nWhy?\n\nWhat evidence supports it?\n\nWhat is missing?\n\nWhat should I do next?\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md\n\nUse:\n\n# Executive Summary\n\n# User Personas\n\n# Design Principles\n\n# Information Architecture\n\n# Navigation\n\n# Case Inbox\n\n# Case Workspace\n\n# Investigation View\n\n# Evidence Explorer\n\n# Header Forensics\n\n# URL Analysis\n\n# Infrastructure Intelligence\n\n# Geolocation\n\n# Attachments\n\n# Historical Intelligence\n\n# Re-investigation\n\n# Forensic Report\n\n# AI Assistant\n\n# Error States\n\n# Loading States\n\n# Empty States\n\n# INCONCLUSIVE State\n\n# Authentication UX\n\n# RBAC-ready UX\n\n# Accessibility\n\n# Responsive Strategy\n\n# Design System\n\n# Component Inventory\n\n# Page Inventory\n\n# User Journeys\n\n# MVP Scope\n\n# Future Scope\n\n# UX Risks\n\n# Final Recommendations\n\nDo NOT write code.\n\nDo NOT invent backend APIs.\n\nThis document will be used as the authoritative frontend product specification.\n\nHere is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/public/assets/aistudio/.gitignore\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/main.tsx\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts\n\nThe following dependencies are installed:\n@google/genai: ^2.4.0\n@tailwindcss/vite: ^4.1.14\n@vitejs/plugin-react: ^5.0.4\ndotenv: ^17.2.3\nexpress: ^4.21.2\nlucide-react: ^0.546.0\nmotion: ^12.23.24\nreact: ^19.0.1\nreact-dom: ^19.0.1\nvite: ^6.2.3\n\nThe following devDependencies are installed:\n@types/express: ^4.17.21\n@types/node: ^22.14.0\nautoprefixer: ^10.4.21\nesbuild: ^0.25.0\ntailwindcss: ^4.1.14\ntsx: ^4.21.0\ntypescript: ~5.8.2\nvite: ^6.2.3\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\"\n\n# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.\n# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.\nAPP_URL=\"MY_APP_URL\"\n\n```\n\n--- /index.html ---\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>My Google AI Studio App</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"My Google AI Studio App\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/App.tsx ---\n```typescript\n/**\n * @license\n * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n */\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <div></div>;\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/index.css ---\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n```\n\n--- /src/main.tsx ---\n```typescript\nimport {StrictMode} from \'react\';\nimport {createRoot} from \'react-dom/client\';\nimport App from \'./App.tsx\';\nimport \'./index.css\';\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')!).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <App />\n  </StrictMode>,\n);\n\n```\n\n--- /tsconfig.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": false,\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"lib\": [\n      \"ES2022\",\n      \"DOM\",\n      \"DOM.Iterable\"\n    ],\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\n        \"./*\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /vite.config.ts ---\n```typescript\nimport tailwindcss from \'@tailwindcss/vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport path from \'path\';\nimport {defineConfig} from \'vite\';\n\nexport default defineConfig(() => {\n  return {\n    plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        \'@\': path.resolve(__dirname, \'.\'),\n      },\n    },\n    server: {\n      // HMR is disabled in AI Studio via DISABLE_HMR env var.\n      // Do not modify\303\242\302\200\302\224file watching is disabled to prevent flickering during agent edits.\n      hmr: process.env.DISABLE_HMR !== \'true\',\n      // Disable file watching when DISABLE_HMR is true to save CPU during agent edits.\n      watch: process.env.DISABLE_HMR === \'true\' ? null : {},\n    },\n  };\n});\n\n```"
    user_requests: "You are a Senior Product Designer, UX Architect, Cybersecurity SOC UX Specialist, and Frontend Product Architect.\n\nWe are building:\n\nASHIELDER\nAI-Powered Email Threat Detection, GeoLocation & Forensic Intelligence Platform\n\nSIH 2026\nPS 26106\n\nThe backend already exists and is being developed separately.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo NOT design or invent backend functionality.\n\nYour job is to research and define the CLIENT-SIDE PRODUCT EXPERIENCE.\n\nThe frontend will be a professional cybersecurity investigation workstation for:\n\n- SOC Analysts\n- Cybersecurity Analysts\n- Digital Forensic Investigators\n- Government / Enterprise Security Teams\n- Incident Response Teams\n\nThe UI must feel like:\n\nProfessional SOC platform\n+\nDigital Forensics workstation\n+\nModern enterprise dashboard\n+\nAI copilot\n\nIt must NOT look like:\n\n- ChatGPT clone\n- generic SaaS dashboard\n- gaming UI\n- hacker/cyberpunk interface\n- overly futuristic interface\n\n==================================================\nOBJECTIVE\n==================================================\n\nResearch and define the complete ASHIELDER client experience.\n\nFocus on:\n\n1. Information Architecture\n2. Navigation\n3. Case Management\n4. Investigation Workspace\n5. Forensic Report\n6. Evidence Explorer\n7. Investigation Journey\n8. Header Forensics\n9. URL Analysis\n10. Infrastructure / IP Intelligence\n11. Geolocation\n12. Attachments\n13. Historical Intelligence\n14. Re-investigation\n15. AI Assistant\n16. Risk / Confidence / Verdict\n17. Error States\n18. Loading States\n19. INCONCLUSIVE State\n20. RBAC-ready UX\n21. Responsive strategy\n22. Accessibility\n\n==================================================\nDESIGN PRINCIPLES\n==================================================\n\nEvidence First.\n\nSummary Before Detail.\n\nCurrent Case \342\211\240 Historical Context.\n\nSystem Finding \342\211\240 AI Explanation.\n\nRisk \342\211\240 Confidence.\n\nMissing Evidence \342\211\240 Safe.\n\nAI is Assistant, not Authority.\n\nInvestigation Journey must be explainable.\n\nForensic evidence must be traceable.\n\nThe analyst must always be able to inspect the source evidence behind an explanation.\n\n==================================================\nMAIN APPLICATION\n==================================================\n\nDesign these primary areas:\n\nAuthentication\n\nCase Inbox\n\nCase Workspace\n\nInvestigation\n\nEvidence Explorer\n\nHistory\n\nReports\n\nAssistant\n\nSettings / Administration\n\n==================================================\nCASE WORKSPACE\n==================================================\n\nDesign the main forensic workspace.\n\nRequired information:\n\nCase ID\nVerdict\nRisk\nConfidence\nSender\nSubject\nReceived Time\nInvestigation Status\n\nKey Findings\n\nInvestigation Journey\n\nEvidence\n\nHistorical Context\n\nInfrastructure\n\nAttachments\n\nAI Assistant\n\nActions:\n\nView Report\nAsk Assistant\nRe-investigate\nEscalate\n\n==================================================\nREPORT EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nDesign a professional forensic report.\n\nInclude:\n\nExecutive Summary\n\nVerdict\n\nRisk\n\nConfidence\n\nKey Findings\n\nAuthentication Analysis\n\nHeader Analysis\n\nReceived Chain\n\nURLs\n\nDomains\n\nIP Intelligence\n\nGeolocation\n\nAttachments\n\nHistorical Context\n\nInvestigation Journey\n\nMissing Evidence\n\nFailed Tools\n\nAudit / Integrity\n\nAI Explanation\n\nMake the difference between:\n\nSYSTEM FINDING\n\nand\n\nAI EXPLANATION\n\nobvious.\n\n==================================================\nAI ASSISTANT\n==================================================\n\nDesign an analyst assistant.\n\nExamples:\n\nWhy is this email suspicious?\n\nWhich evidence is strongest?\n\nWhat evidence is missing?\n\nWhy is confidence low?\n\nWhat should I investigate next?\n\nWhat historical cases are related?\n\nThe assistant must cite evidence IDs.\n\nIt must never visually appear to control:\n\nRisk\nConfidence\nVerdict\n\n==================================================\nVISUAL SYSTEM\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nTypography\nSpacing\nGrid\nCards\nTables\nBadges\nStatus styles\nIcons\nButtons\nTabs\nTimeline\nMap\nEvidence chips\nRisk visualization\nConfidence visualization\n\nUse a professional, sober cybersecurity aesthetic.\n\nAvoid excessive gradients and neon colors.\n\n==================================================\nLOW COGNITIVE LOAD\n==================================================\n\nThe analyst should understand a case within seconds.\n\nPrioritize:\n\nWhat happened?\n\nHow dangerous is it?\n\nWhy?\n\nWhat evidence supports it?\n\nWhat is missing?\n\nWhat should I do next?\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT\n==================================================\n\nCreate:\n\nASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md\n\nUse:\n\n# Executive Summary\n\n# User Personas\n\n# Design Principles\n\n# Information Architecture\n\n# Navigation\n\n# Case Inbox\n\n# Case Workspace\n\n# Investigation View\n\n# Evidence Explorer\n\n# Header Forensics\n\n# URL Analysis\n\n# Infrastructure Intelligence\n\n# Geolocation\n\n# Attachments\n\n# Historical Intelligence\n\n# Re-investigation\n\n# Forensic Report\n\n# AI Assistant\n\n# Error States\n\n# Loading States\n\n# Empty States\n\n# INCONCLUSIVE State\n\n# Authentication UX\n\n# RBAC-ready UX\n\n# Accessibility\n\n# Responsive Strategy\n\n# Design System\n\n# Component Inventory\n\n# Page Inventory\n\n# User Journeys\n\n# MVP Scope\n\n# Future Scope\n\n# UX Risks\n\n# Final Recommendations\n\nDo NOT write code.\n\nDo NOT invent backend APIs.\n\nThis document will be used as the authoritative frontend product specification."
    user_requests: "ASHIELDER Backend E2E Trace\nPhase 2 \342\200\224 End-to-End Execution Traces Generated: 2026-09-05 Evidence Standard: All names are ACTUAL file paths, classes, and functions observed during runtime.\n\nCASE 1 \342\200\224 BENIGN EMAIL\nInput: Clean internal email, SPF=pass, DKIM=pass, DMARC=pass, matching Reply-To/Return-Path, no URLs, no attachments.\n\n\nPOST /api/v1/investigations/analyze [NO AUTH]\n  \342\206\222 app/api/v1/investigation.py:analyze_email()\n  \342\206\222 EmailParser.parse(raw_bytes)\n     app/services/ingestion/parser.py\n     \342\206\222 ParsedEmail (headers extracted, no URLs, no attachments)\n  \342\206\222 EvidenceNormalizer.normalize(parsed)\n     app/services/evidence/normalizer.py\n     \342\206\222 EmailEvidencePackage (clean auth, no mismatches)\n  \342\206\222 InvestigationDecisionEngine.run_stage1(package)\n     app/services/investigation/decision_engine.py:120\n     \342\206\222 init_state() \342\206\222 InvestigationState + L0 facts:\n       auth_spf_result=pass, auth_dkim_result=pass, auth_dmarc_result=pass\n     \342\206\222 FastHistoricalLookup.perform_lookup() [wrapped in bare except]\n     \342\206\222 AuditLogger.log_investigation_started()\n     \342\206\222 initialize_to_level0(state)\n     \342\206\222 generate_initial_hypotheses() \342\206\222 13 hypotheses, all low scores\n     \342\206\222 run_adaptive_loop()\n       Iteration 0 (L0): deterministic tools\n         \342\206\222 spf_dkim_dmarc, header_parse, relay_reconstruction, deterministic_heuristics\n         \342\206\222 All auth pass \342\206\222 negative/zero risk weights\n         \342\206\222 RiskEngine.assess(): risk_score low\n       Level advances: L0\342\206\222L1\342\206\222L2\342\206\222L3 (exhausting candidates)\n       \342\206\222 Stop: NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS\n     \342\206\222 sm_status=STOPPED (not COMPLETED \342\200\224 known issue)\n  \342\206\222 to_investigation_result(stage1)\n     app/services/investigation/result_factory.py\n  \342\206\222 InvestigationResult:\n     verdict: BENIGN or INCONCLUSIVE\n     risk_score: low\n     tools_used: [spf_dkim_dmarc, header_parse, ...]\n     tools_failed: [nlp_intent_ml] (if minimal body content)\n  \342\206\222 Persistence: best-effort (fails silently if DB unreachable)\n  \342\206\222 Audit: chain valid, records created\nRuntime Finding: State machine ends at STOPPED (not COMPLETED) because NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS fires after L3 with groq_reasoning disabled. This causes test_clean_email_sm_status_is_completed to FAIL. The investigation still produces a correct verdict.\n\nCASE 2 \342\200\224 PHISHING EMAIL\nInput: Fake bank email, SPF=fail, DKIM=fail, DMARC=fail, Reply-To mismatch, phishing URL in HTML, urgency language.\n\n\n  \342\206\222 EmailParser.parse(raw_bytes)\n     \342\206\222 URLs extracted from HTML: http://bankofamerica-secure.net/verify?id=12345\n     \342\206\222 Link/display mismatch detected (displays bankofamerica.com/secure)\n     \342\206\222 ParsedEmail with urls, html body\n  \342\206\222 EvidenceNormalizer.normalize(parsed)\n     \342\206\222 IPIndicator: 45.33.32.156 (public)\n     \342\206\222 DomainIndicator: bankofamerica-secure.net, freemail.com, evil-server.com\n     \342\206\222 URLItem: http://bankofamerica-secure.net/verify\n     \342\206\222 Auth failures: SPF=fail, DKIM=fail, DMARC=fail\n     \342\206\222 Reply-To mismatch: hacker@freemail.com vs security@bankofamerica-secure.net\n     \342\206\222 Return-Path mismatch: bounce@evil-server.com\n  \342\206\222 InvestigationDecisionEngine.run_stage1(package)\n     \342\206\222 init_state() \342\206\222 L0 facts (7 items):\n       [1] auth_spf_result=fail (FACT, AUTHENTICATION)\n       [2] auth_dkim_result=fail (FACT, AUTHENTICATION)\n       [3] auth_dmarc_result=fail (FACT, AUTHENTICATION)\n       [4] reply_to_domain_mismatch (FACT, HEADER)\n       [5] return_path_domain_mismatch (FACT, HEADER)\n       [6] received_hop_count (FACT, RELAY)\n       [7] url_http_scheme (FACT, URL)\n     \342\206\222 Hypotheses scored: credential_phishing=HIGH, spoofing=HIGH, malicious_url=HIGH\n     \342\206\222 run_adaptive_loop():\n       Iteration 0 (L0):\n         Tools: spf_dkim_dmarc, header_parse, relay_reconstruction,\n                deterministic_heuristics, url_canonicalizer, ip_classifier\n         Evidence [8-14]: HEURISTIC auth results, reply_to mismatch,\n                          message_id mismatch, relay_anomaly\n         Risk rising: DMARC fail +40, SPF fail +20, DKIM fail +15\n       Iteration 1 (L1):\n         Tools: nlp_intent_ml, url_ml\n         Evidence [15]: ML_SIGNAL intent=PHISHING (M2, conf=0.80)\n         Evidence [16-20]: HEURISTIC urgency_score, se_flag_urgency,\n                           se_flag_fear_trigger, mentioned_organization x2\n         Evidence [21-22]: ML_SIGNAL url_ml_risk per URL (M4 LightURLNet)\n       Iteration 2+ (L2):\n         Tools: geoip_lookup, sender_history, rdap_domain_lookup, url_reputation\n         Evidence [23-41]: FACT geolocation (country, ASN, ISP, coords, etc.)\n         Evidence [42]: FACT historical_cases_found\n       \342\206\222 Stop: NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS or CONFIDENCE_TARGET\n  \342\206\222 InvestigationResult:\n     verdict: SUSPICIOUS (observed) or MALICIOUS\n     risk_score: high\n     confidence: 0.70+ (multiple corroborating evidence types)\n     tools_used: [spf_dkim_dmarc, header_parse, relay_reconstruction,\n                  deterministic_heuristics, url_canonicalizer, ip_classifier,\n                  nlp_intent_ml, url_ml, geoip_lookup, sender_history, ...]\n     tools_failed: []\n     evidence: 42 items total\n  \342\206\222 Audit: valid chain, multi-iteration records\nRuntime Finding: Full pipeline confirmed working. 42 evidence items with correct typing. FACT from parser, HEURISTIC from handlers, ML_SIGNAL from M2/M4, GeoIP FACTs from external. Zero duplicates. Zero ML-as-FACT violations.\n\nCASE 3 \342\200\224 BEC / IMPERSONATION\nInput: CEO impersonation, wire transfer request, Reply-To=gmail.com, DKIM=none, DMARC=none, urgency language.\n\n\n  \342\206\222 EmailParser.parse()\n     From: CEO John Smith <ceo@company-inc.com>\n     Reply-To: john.smith.ceo@gmail.com (MISMATCH)\n     Auth: SPF=pass, DKIM=none, DMARC=none\n     No URLs, no attachments. Urgency: \"urgent\", \"time-sensitive\", \"confidential\"\n  \342\206\222 EvidenceNormalizer.normalize()\n     \342\206\222 Reply-To domain mismatch flagged\n     \342\206\222 DKIM=none, DMARC=none \342\206\222 flagged as missing\n  \342\206\222 InvestigationDecisionEngine.run_stage1()\n     \342\206\222 L0 facts: dmarc=none, reply_to_mismatch\n     \342\206\222 Hypotheses: BEC=HIGH, executive_impersonation=HIGH\n     \342\206\222 L0: header_parse \342\206\222 mismatch detection\n     \342\206\222 L0: deterministic_heuristics \342\206\222 urgency keyword detection\n     \342\206\222 L1: nlp_intent_ml \342\206\222 intent classification\n     \342\206\222 Risk: moderate (reply-to mismatch + DMARC none + urgency)\n     \342\206\222 Verdict: SUSPICIOUS\nCASE 4 \342\200\224 ATTACHMENT EMAIL\nInput: Email with PDF attachment, clean auth, no suspicious URLs.\n\n\n  \342\206\222 EmailParser.parse()\n     Multipart MIME: text/plain + application/pdf\n     Attachment: invoice.pdf (SHA-256 computed)\n     File signature detection via file_signatures.py (magic bytes: %PDF)\n     Auth: SPF=pass, DKIM=pass, DMARC=pass\n  \342\206\222 EvidenceNormalizer.normalize()\n     \342\206\222 AttachmentRef with hash, filename, content_type\n     \342\206\222 Clean auth indicators\n  \342\206\222 InvestigationDecisionEngine.run_stage1()\n     \342\206\222 L0: attachment_hasher \342\206\222 SHA-256 IOC\n     \342\206\222 Hypotheses: malware_delivery, malicious_attachment scored\n     \342\206\222 attachment_static_analysis: DISABLED (enabled=False in registry)\n     \342\206\222 Risk: low (clean auth, no suspicious signals)\n     \342\206\222 Verdict: BENIGN or INCONCLUSIVE\nRuntime Finding: Attachment hashing works. File signature detection works. But attachment_static_analysis is disabled, so deep attachment analysis does not occur.\n\nCASE 5 \342\200\224 INCOMPLETE EMAIL\nInput: Minimal headers \342\200\224 no Authentication-Results, no Received, no Message-ID, no Date.\n\n\n  \342\206\222 EmailParser.parse()\n     Minimal: From, To, Subject only\n     No Authentication-Results \342\206\222 empty auth\n     No Received \342\206\222 empty relay chain\n  \342\206\222 EvidenceNormalizer.normalize()\n     \342\206\222 Sparse package: no auth, no IPs, minimal domains\n  \342\206\222 InvestigationDecisionEngine.run_stage1()\n     \342\206\222 L0 facts: very few (minimal signals)\n     \342\206\222 Hypotheses: all low scores (insufficient data)\n     \342\206\222 Adaptive loop: few tools have inputs to work with\n     \342\206\222 nlp_intent_ml: \"normalisation produced zero evidence items\"\n     \342\206\222 Risk: 0.0, Confidence: 0.0000\n     \342\206\222 Stop: NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS\n     \342\206\222 Verdict: INCONCLUSIVE\nRuntime Finding: CONFIRMED verdict=INCONCLUSIVE, risk=0.0, confidence=0.0000. Missing evidence: \"Tool nlp_intent_ml failed: normalisation produced zero evidence items\". Correctly follows the principle that insufficient evidence \342\206\222 INCONCLUSIVE, never BENIGN.\n\nCASE 6 \342\200\224 HISTORICAL MATCH\nInput: Email from sender/domain/IP with previous investigation history.\n\n\n  \342\206\222 InvestigationDecisionEngine.run_stage1()\n     \342\206\222 Step 1b: FastHistoricalLookup.perform_lookup(package)\n       app/services/investigation/historical_lookup.py\n       \342\206\222 HistoricalRepository.find_historical_indicators()\n       \342\206\222 If match: EvidenceItem(type=HISTORICAL) created\n       \342\206\222 If DB error: except Exception: pass [SILENT \342\200\224 P0-4]\n     \342\206\222 L2 tools:\n       sender_history \342\206\222 historical_cases_found evidence\n       historical_correlation \342\206\222 deep cross-indicator correlation\n     \342\206\222 Historical evidence influences hypothesis re-scoring\n     \342\206\222 Historical confidence cap: 0.70 (confidence.py:106-112)\n       If only historical evidence is strong \342\206\222 conf capped at 0.70\nRuntime Finding: E2E trace shows historical_cases_found evidence produced by sender_history tool with correct typing (source_type=threat_intel_api). Historical confidence cap confirmed in source code.\n\nCASE 7 \342\200\224 TOOL FAILURE\nInput: Email with URLs, but M4 model unavailable for url_ml tool.\n\n\n  \342\206\222 run_adaptive_loop()\n     \342\206\222 url_ml selected for URL analysis\n     \342\206\222 ToolExecutor.execute()\n       \342\206\222 handler returns {\"status\": \"failed\", \"error\": \"M4 model unavailable\"}\n     \342\206\222 ToolExecutionResult(status=FAILED)\n     \342\206\222 NO ML_SIGNAL evidence produced (correct behavior)\n     \342\206\222 missing_evidence: [\"Tool url_ml failed: status=FAILED error=M4 model unavailable\"]\n     \342\206\222 tools_failed: [\"url_ml\"]\n     \342\206\222 Confidence penalty: failure_factor reduces confidence\n     \342\206\222 Resource-stop guard: if only remaining evidence \342\206\222 INCONCLUSIVE (not BENIGN)\nRuntime Finding (from test output): CONFIRMED. tools_failed=[\"url_ml\"], missing_evidence populated, ml_signals=[], confidence=0.7087 (reduced from what it would be). Tool failure correctly does NOT produce clean/benign evidence. Verdict=SUSPICIOUS (not BENIGN).\n\nCASE 8 \342\200\224 INCONCLUSIVE (Resource Exhaustion)\nInput: Complex email that exhausts budget before confidence target.\n\n\n  \342\206\222 run_adaptive_loop()\n     Budget: max_latency_ms=4000, max_tool_calls=8, max_external_calls=4\n     \342\206\222 Iterations consume budget\n     \342\206\222 should_stop() \342\206\222 StopReason.BUDGET_EXHAUSTED or NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS\n     \342\206\222 assign_verdict_with_stop_guard(risk, confidence, stop_reason)\n       app/services/risk/verdict.py:101-133\n       \342\206\222 If verdict=BENIGN + resource-stop: override \342\206\222 INCONCLUSIVE\n       \342\206\222 If verdict=MALICIOUS/SUSPICIOUS: keep as-is\n     \342\206\222 InvestigationResult._resource_stop_cannot_be_benign()\n       app/contracts/result.py \342\200\224 contract-level enforcement\n       \342\206\222 resource_stop + BENIGN = raises ValueError\nRuntime Finding: INCONCLUSIVE validation confirms correct behavior. E2E test for incomplete email: verdict=INCONCLUSIVE, risk=0.0, confidence=0.0000, stop_reason=NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS. Resource-stop guard confirmed in both verdict.py and result.py contract validator.ASHIELDER Backend Runtime Matrix\nPhase 2 \342\200\224 Expected vs Observed Behavior Generated: 2026-09-05\n\nComponent\tExpected\tObserved\tStatus\tEvidence\nIngestion\tAccept .eml via POST, validate size/name\tAccepts file, validates empty/missing. No auth on investigation endpoint.\t\360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\tAPI test: 422 for missing file, 400 for empty, ReadTimeout for valid (DB hang)\nParsing\tParse MIME headers, body, attachments, URLs\tParser extracts all components correctly. ParsedEmail contract enforced (frozen, extra=forbid).\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\t318 parser-related tests pass. E2E trace shows correct extraction.\nEvidence\tTyped EvidenceItem with provenance, structural guards\t42 evidence items for phishing email. 0 duplicates. 0 ML-as-FACT violations. Types correctly separated.\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\tE2E evidence flow trace. Validators enforce type constraints.\nForensics\tSPF/DKIM/DMARC, header mismatches, relay analysis\tAll L0 tools execute. Auth results parsed. Mismatches detected. Relay anomalies detected.\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\tEvidence trace: auth_spf_result, auth_dkim_result, auth_dmarc_result, reply_to_domain_mismatch, relay_anomaly\nHistory\tHistorical IOC lookup; silent error swallowing\tFast historical lookup runs. Bare except Exception: pass confirmed. Sender history tool produces evidence.\t\360\237\237\241 PARTIAL (P0 issue)\tEvidence trace: historical_cases_found. Code confirms bare except at decision_engine.py:177.\nAdaptive Investigation\tMulti-iteration loop with tool re-selection, budget, level advancement\tLoop genuinely iterates. Tools ranked per hypothesis. Levels advance L0\342\206\222L1\342\206\222L2. Budget tracked. Stop conditions evaluate.\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\tE2E: iteration_count, tools_used, tools_failed populated. 318 tests pass including adaptive loop tests.\nTools\t16 enabled tools with handler resolution\t16 enabled handlers resolve via importlib. Executor classifies success/failure. Failed tools recorded.\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\tExecutor tests pass. E2E shows tools_used and tools_failed.\nML (M2)\tDistilBERT intent classification via ONNX\tModel loads at startup (267MB). Produces ML_SIGNAL with intent label (PHISHING/MALWARE/SPAM/BENIGN).\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\tEvidence trace: intent key, type=ML_SIGNAL, source_type=ml_model, producer=M2IntentClassifier\nML (M4)\tLightURLNet URL classification via ONNX\tModel loads (1MB). Produces url_ml_risk ML_SIGNAL per URL.\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\tEvidence trace: url_ml_risk, type=ML_SIGNAL, source_type=ml_model, producer=M4LightUrlNetInference\nRisk\tDeterministic weight table, category caps, 0-100\tRisk scores computed. Category caps applied. Reputation dedup works.\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\t318 passed tests include risk engine tests. E2E shows bounded values.\nVerdict\tMALICIOUS/SUSPICIOUS/BENIGN/INCONCLUSIVE with resource-stop guard\tVerdict correctly assigned. Resource-stop guard prevents BENIGN on exhaustion.\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\tINCONCLUSIVE for incomplete email (risk=0, conf=0, stop=NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS).\nReporting\tInline report in API; standalone report_generator unused\tInline report at investigation.py:193-335. GET /report \342\206\222 500 (DB hang). report_generator.py never called.\t\360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\tAPI test: 500 on report endpoint. Code review: no callers for report_generator.py.\nAudit\tHash-chained append-only, tamper detection\tRecords created with SHA-256 chain. verify_chain() works. frozen=True prevents mutation.\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED\tE2E: audit record counts, valid chain. Frozen contract blocks mutation.\nDatabase\tPostgreSQL (prod) / SQLite (test)\tTests use SQLite (StaticPool). Prod DB unreachable (db:5432). init_db() called inline on requests.\t\360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\t318 tests pass with SQLite. Live API hangs on DB ops (socket.gaierror).\nAPI\t9 endpoints, 7 functional, 2 empty\tHealth: 200. Investigation: hangs on DB. Email: 401 without auth. Reinvestigate: 501. Nonexistent: 500.\t\360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\tAPI validation script covers all endpoints.\nAuth\tFirebase on email; NONE on investigation\tEmail: 401 without auth. Investigation: accepts all requests with no auth check whatsoever.\t\360\237\224\264 BROKEN\tNo auth, fake bearer, wrong API key \342\206\222 all accepted on investigation endpoint.\nRBAC\tNo user model, roles, or permissions\tNo role concepts. No authorization checks. Any client accesses any resource.\t\360\237\224\264 ABSENT\tNo User model, no role enum, no permission decorator in codebase.\nCORS\tNo CORSMiddleware configured\tOPTIONS \342\206\222 405. No Access-Control headers. Browser frontends blocked.\t\360\237\224\264 BROKEN\tOPTIONS: 405, ACAO=MISSING, ACAM=MISSING.\nError Handling\tNo global exception handler\tNonexistent resources \342\206\222 raw 500 \"Internal Server Error\". No structured error.\t\360\237\224\264 BROKEN\tGET /investigations/nonexistent \342\206\222 500 plain text.\nRate Limiting\tIn-memory rate limiter exists but not injected\t15 rapid requests: no 429. Rate limiter in auth.py never injected.\t\360\237\224\264 ABSENT\tNo 429 after 15 requests. auth.py rate limiter unused.\nRaw .EML Preservation\tNot preserved\tSHA-256 hash stored but raw bytes not persisted. Cannot recover original.\t\360\237\224\264 MISSING\tParsedEmail.sha256 exists; raw_artifact_reference is hash, not bytes.ASHIELDER Backend Final E2E Validation\nPhase 4 \342\200\224 End-to-End Pipeline Verification Generated: 2026-09-05\n\nValidation Method\nAll cases verified via pytest test suite (358 tests). Representative cases traced through the full pipeline:\n\n\n.eml \342\206\222 Parser \342\206\222 Evidence \342\206\222 Forensics \342\206\222 History \342\206\222 Hypotheses \342\206\222\nAdaptive Investigation \342\206\222 Tools \342\206\222 ML \342\206\222 Risk \342\206\222 Confidence \342\206\222 Verdict \342\206\222 Report \342\206\222 Audit\nCase Results\n#\tCase\tAPI\tRisk\tConfidence\tVerdict\tEvidence Count\tLevels\tReport\tAudit\tStatus\n1\tBenign (all auth pass)\tPOST /analyze\t< 40\t> 0.5\tBENIGN / INCONCLUSIVE\t> 10\tL0 \342\206\222 L1\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205 hash chain\t\342\234\205 PASS\n2\tPhishing (auth fail + reply-to mismatch)\tPOST /analyze\t> 0\t> 0\t\342\211\240 BENIGN\t> 10\tL0 \342\206\222 L1 \342\206\222 L2\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205 hash chain\t\342\234\205 PASS\n3\tBEC (SPF pass + BEC content)\tUnit test\t> 0\t\342\200\224\t\342\211\240 BENIGN\t3+\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\234\205 PASS\n4\tAttachment (hash extraction)\tUnit test\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\thash present\tL0\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\234\205 PASS\n5\tIncomplete headers\tParser test\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\tpartial evidence\tL0\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\234\205 PASS\n6\tUnknown IOC\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\232\252 NOT TESTED\n7\tHistorical match\tUnit test\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\thistorical facts\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\n8\tTool failure (url_ml unavailable)\tMonkeypatch test\t\342\200\224\t> 0\t\342\211\240 BENIGN\ttools_failed=[\"url_ml\"]\tL0 \342\206\222 L1\t\342\200\224\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205 PASS\n9\tInconclusive (budget exhaustion)\tUnit test\t5.0\t0.3\tINCONCLUSIVE\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\234\205 PASS\n10\tReinvestigation\tAPI test\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\342\200\224\t\360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\nPipeline Stage Verification\nStage 1: Parser (L0)\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\nEvidence: 5 .eml fixture files parsed correctly\nTests: test_parser.py, test_evidence_normalizer.py\nVerified: Subject, From, To, Message-ID, Authentication-Results, Received headers, body, URLs, attachments\nStage 2: Evidence Normalization\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\nEvidence: EmailEvidencePackage correctly constructed with typed indicators\nVerified: URL deduplication, domain extraction, attachment hashing, sender/recipient evidence\nStage 3: Forensics (L0 Tools)\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\nTools: spf_dkim_dmarc, header_parse, relay_reconstruction, deterministic_heuristics, url_canonicalizer, ip_classifier, attachment_hasher\nVerified: All L0 tools execute deterministically, evidence accumulates correctly\nStage 4: Historical Lookup\nStatus: \360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\nImplementation: FastHistoricalLookup queries DB for matching indicators\nVerified: Non-blocking behavior (failure doesn\'t halt investigation), structured logging on failure\nNot verified: Real historical match against persisted data (requires Supabase)\nStage 5: Hypothesis Generation\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\nEvidence: All 13 AttackHypothesisType values generated for every email\nVerified: Profiles selected, relevance scored, hypotheses ranked\nStage 6: Adaptive Investigation (L1 \342\206\222 L2)\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\nEvidence: run_adaptive_loop genuinely selects tools, executes them, accumulates evidence\nVerified: Budget tracking, tool deduplication, level escalation, stop conditions (NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS, CONFIDENCE_TARGET_REACHED, BUDGET_EXHAUSTED)\nStage 7: ML Models\nM2 (DistilBERT intent classifier): \342\234\205 CONFIRMED \342\200\224 labels: 0=PHISHING, 1=MALWARE, 2=SPAM, 3=BENIGN\nM4 (LightURLNet): \342\234\205 CONFIRMED \342\200\224 URL threat scoring with real model weights\nVerified: Model loading, adapter integration, tool execution, ML_SIGNAL evidence type\nStage 8: Threat Intelligence\nStatus: \360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\nRegistered & enabled: dns_resolver, rdap_domain_lookup, url_reputation, geoip_lookup, ip_geolocation, ip_reputation, sender_history, url_ml, nlp_intent_ml\nDisabled: urlhaus_feed_lookup, html_analysis, attachment_static_analysis, domain_intelligence, ip_intelligence\nExternal providers: VirusTotal, AbuseIPDB, IPINFO \342\200\224 configured but NOT runtime-tested (requires API keys + network)\nStage 9: Risk / Confidence / Verdict\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\nRisk: 0\342\200\223100 bounded, evidence-weighted scoring\nConfidence: 0\342\200\2231 bounded, evidence-count and tool-coverage based\nVerdict: assign_verdict_with_stop_guard handles resource stops \342\206\222 INCONCLUSIVE\nVerified: BEC with SPF pass produces net positive risk; budget exhaustion \342\206\222 INCONCLUSIVE; tool failure \342\211\240 BENIGN\nStage 10: Reporting\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\nImplementation: ReportGenerator.generate_report() with Groq fallback\nVerified: Report endpoint no longer 501, Groq unavailability degrades gracefully to deterministic report\nStage 11: Audit Chain\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\nImplementation: Hash-chained audit records with GENESIS_HASH\nVerified: compute_record_hash() produces deterministic hashes, chain integrity tested\nEvidence Integrity Verification (Phase 4N)\nCheck\tResult\nNo duplicate evidence IDs\t\342\234\205 Verified by evidence normalization tests\nProvenance exists\t\342\234\205 Every EvidenceItem has provenance field\nML_SIGNAL stays ML_SIGNAL\t\342\234\205 source_type=ML_MODEL enforced\nHEURISTIC stays HEURISTIC\t\342\234\205 Type preservation verified\nTool failure \342\211\240 clean result\t\342\234\205 tools_failed list populated, verdict \342\211\240 BENIGN\nEvidence not overwritten\t\342\234\205 Append-only evidence accumulation\nReinvestigation Verification (Phase 4T)\nCheck\tResult\nEndpoint returns 501\t\342\235\214 No \342\200\224 fully implemented (was 501 pre-Phase 3)\nOriginal immutable\t\342\234\205 New case_id generated\nLineage reference\t\342\234\205 original_case_id field in CaseModel\nFull pipeline re-run\t\342\234\205 Calls _engine.run_stage1()\nPersistence\t\342\234\205 Best-effort (same as analyze_email)\nRuntime tested\t\360\237\237\241 Requires DB with existing case for original lookup\n ASHIELDER Backend Final Audit\nPhase 4 \342\200\224 Final Validation & Release Audit Generated: 2026-09-05\n\n1. Executive Summary\nASHIELDER\'s backend has completed four phases of engineering:\n\nPhase 1: Discovery (60+ modules mapped, 9 API endpoints catalogued)\nPhase 2: Runtime validation (330 tests, 318 passing, 10 failing, pipeline proven real)\nPhase 3: Fix/hardening (auth, CORS, rate limiting, error handling, reinvestigation, historical logging)\nPhase 4: Final audit (all fixes verified, 358 tests passing, live API validation complete)\nThe backend is SIH demo ready. It is not production ready due to unverified Supabase persistence, missing RBAC, and secrets requiring rotation.\n\n2. Final Backend Status\nMetric\tPhase 2\tPhase 3\tPhase 4\nTotal tests\t330\t358\t358\nPassed\t318\t358\t358\nFailed\t10\t0\t0\nSkipped\t2\t2\t2\nErrors\t0\t0\t0\nNet improvement: +40 tests added, all 10 previous failures fixed, 0 regressions.\n\n3. What Was Built\nThe ASHIELDER backend is a fully deterministic email threat investigation engine:\n\nParser: Extracts headers, body, URLs, attachments from raw .eml bytes\nEvidence normalizer: Produces typed EmailEvidencePackage with provenance tracking\n16 investigation tools: 7 L0 deterministic + 4 L1 ML + 5 L2 enrichment\n2 ML models: M2 (DistilBERT intent classifier), M4 (LightURLNet URL scorer)\nAdaptive investigation loop: Multi-level tool selection with budget tracking\nRisk engine: Evidence-weighted scoring (0\342\200\223100) with confidence (0\342\200\2231)\nVerdict logic: Stop-guard prevents BENIGN on resource exhaustion\nAudit chain: Hash-chained records with genesis hash\nReporting: Groq-enhanced with deterministic fallback\nReinvestigation: Full re-run with lineage tracking\n4. What Was Verified (Phase 4)\nArea\tMethod\tResult\nBackend startup\tLive server start on port 8001\t\342\234\205 Clean\nML model loading\tStartup log inspection\t\342\234\205 M2 + M4 loaded\nRoute registration\tFastAPI routes enumerated\t\342\234\205 All 6 endpoints\nAuthentication (7 tests)\tLive API requests\t\342\234\205 All 401 correct\nRate limiting\t12 rapid requests\t\342\234\205 10 x 429\nCORS\tOPTIONS preflight\t\342\234\205 200, headers correct\nRequest ID\tHeader inspection\t\342\234\205 UUID generated, caller ID echoed\nError handling\tNonexistent resource\t\342\234\205 Structured JSON, no leak\nTest suite\tFull pytest run\t\342\234\205 358 passed, 0 failed\nDB lifecycle\tSource inspection\t\342\234\205 init at startup only\nHistorical error handling\tSource inspection\t\342\234\205 Structured logging, non-blocking\nSecret scan\tgrep across working tree\t\342\234\205 Clean (no hardcoded secrets in code)\n5. What Remains Partial\nArea\tWhat Works\tWhat Doesn\'t\nHistorical intelligence\tImplementation complete, non-blocking, logged\tRequires PostgreSQL for runtime matching\nThreat intelligence\t9 tools registered+enabled\t3 disabled (handler unavailable), external providers not runtime-tested\nDatabase persistence\tSQLite test mode works\tSupabase PostgreSQL not connected (placeholder REGION in URL)\nReinvestigation\tFull pipeline implementation\tRequires existing case in DB for original lookup\n6. What Remains Missing\nArea\tStatus\tSeverity\nRaw .eml preservation (Supabase Storage)\tNOT IMPLEMENTED\tP1\nRBAC (user model, roles, permissions)\tDEFERRED\tP1\nGit history secret cleanup\tREQUIRES ROTATION\tP0 (production)\nDistributed rate limiting\tIN-MEMORY ONLY\tP2\n7. What Was Fixed in Phase 3\nFix\tFiles Changed\tTests Added\nAuth on all endpoints\tinvestigation.py\ttest_auth.py (10 tests)\nCORS middleware\tmain.py, config.py\ttest_cors.py (8 tests)\nRate limiting injected\tAlready in verify_api_key\ttest_auth.py::TestRateLimiting\nGlobal exception handler\tmain.py\ttest_error_handling.py (10 tests)\nRequest ID middleware\tmiddleware/request_id.py (NEW)\ttest_cors.py::TestRequestIDHeader\nHistorical bare except\tdecision_engine.py\tExisting tests pass\nReinvestigation endpoint\tinvestigation.py (was 501)\ttest_auth.py::test_reinvestigate_*\nDB lifecycle (init at startup)\tmain.py, decision_engine.py\tconftest.py fixture\n.env.example template\t.env.example (NEW)\t\342\200\224\n10 stale test assertions\t4 test files\t\342\200\224\n8. Phase 4 Regression Fixes\nFix\tRoot Cause\tChange\nGeoIP L2 candidate test\tSPOOFING profile uses ip_geolocation not geoip_lookup\tUpdated assertion to correct tool name\nPer-request init_db() in decision_engine\tRemoved from API handlers in Phase 3 but remained in decision_engine\tRemoved; added session-scoped init_db() in conftest.py\nconftest.py DB initialization\tIn-memory SQLite tables not created for TestClient tests\tAdded init_test_database session-scoped fixture\n9. API Status\nEndpoint\tAuth\tStatus\nGET /health\tNone\t\342\234\205 200\nPOST /api/v1/investigations/analyze\tX-API-Key\t\342\234\205 200\nGET /api/v1/investigations/{id}\tX-API-Key\t\342\234\205 200/404\nGET /api/v1/investigations/{id}/evidence\tX-API-Key\t\342\234\205 200/404\nGET /api/v1/investigations/{id}/report\tX-API-Key\t\342\234\205 200/404\nPOST /api/v1/investigations/{id}/reinvestigate\tX-API-Key\t\342\234\205 200/404/422\nNo endpoints return fake/mock/placeholder data. No 501 stubs remain.\n\n10. Authentication\nMechanism: X-API-Key header compared against settings.SECRET_KEY\nInjection: Depends(verify_api_key) on all 5 investigation endpoints\nLive verification: 7/7 auth tests pass (no auth \342\206\222 401, wrong key \342\206\222 401, valid \342\206\222 200)\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n11. RBAC\nCurrent: Single shared API key \342\200\224 all authenticated clients have equal access\nAcceptable for: Single-tenant SIH MVP demo\nNot acceptable for: Multi-user or multi-tenant deployment\nMigration path: Add User model, JWT claims, resource-level authorization\nStatus: \360\237\237\241 DEFERRED\n12. Supabase PostgreSQL\nDATABASE_URL: postgresql+asyncpg://postgres:***@db.rwpbehltkjoixiadlcdr.supabase.co:5432/postgres\nImplementation: Alembic wired, initial migration complete.\nConnection: Direct connection db.rwpbehltkjoixiadlcdr.supabase.co tested and works.\nStatus: \342\234\205 VERIFIED\n13. Supabase Storage\nConfig: SUPABASE_BUCKET_NAME added to settings\nImplementation: NOT IMPLEMENTED\nStatus: \360\237\224\264 DEFERRED\n14. Raw EML Preservation\nSHA-256 computed: \342\234\205 Yes (email_sha256 in EmailEvidencePackage)\nOriginal bytes stored: \342\235\214 No storage backend\nBytes recoverable: \342\235\214 No\nStatus: \360\237\224\264 NOT IMPLEMENTED\n15. Evidence Integrity\nTyped evidence: \342\234\205 EvidenceType (FACT, HEURISTIC, ML_SIGNAL, HISTORICAL)\nProvenance tracking: \342\234\205 Every EvidenceItem has Provenance\nNo duplicate IDs: \342\234\205 Verified\nTool failure recorded: \342\234\205 tools_failed list populated\nML_SIGNAL source type: \342\234\205 Enforced as SourceType.ML_MODEL\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n16. Header Forensics\nSPF/DKIM/DMARC parsing: \342\234\205 Working\nReceived header reconstruction: \342\234\205 Relay chain traced\nReply-To mismatch detection: \342\234\205 Working\nReturn-Path mismatch: \342\234\205 Working\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n17. Adaptive Investigation\nImplementation: Genuine multi-level tool selection (L0 \342\206\222 L1 \342\206\222 L2)\nBudget tracking: \342\234\205 tool_calls, external_calls, latency_ms, llm_tokens\nTool deduplication: \342\234\205 URL canonicalization, domain deduplication\nStop conditions: NO_CANDIDATE_TOOLS, CONFIDENCE_TARGET_REACHED, BUDGET_EXHAUSTED\nEvidence accumulation: \342\234\205 Per-iteration, append-only\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n18. ML\nModel\tType\tStatus\nM2 IntentClassifier\tDistilBERT (4 labels)\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED \342\200\224 loaded at startup\nM4 LightURLNet\tCNN URL scorer\t\342\234\205 CONFIRMED \342\200\224 loaded at startup\n19. Threat Intelligence\nTool\tLevel\tEnabled\tRuntime Tested\nspf_dkim_dmarc\tL0\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\nheader_parse\tL0\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\nrelay_reconstruction\tL0\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\ndeterministic_heuristics\tL0\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\nurl_canonicalizer\tL0\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\nip_classifier\tL0\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\nattachment_hasher\tL0\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\nnlp_intent_ml\tL1\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\nurl_ml\tL1\t\342\234\205\t\342\234\205\ndns_resolver\tL2\t\342\234\205\t\360\237\237\241\nrdap_domain_lookup\tL2\t\342\234\205\t\360\237\237\241\nurl_reputation\tL2\t\342\234\205\t\360\237\237\241\ngeoip_lookup\tL2\t\342\234\205\t\360\237\237\241\nip_geolocation\tL2\t\342\234\205\t\360\237\237\241\nip_reputation\tL2\t\342\234\205\t\360\237\237\241\nsender_history\tL2\t\342\234\205\t\360\237\237\241\ndomain_intelligence\tL2\t\342\235\214\t\342\235\214\nip_intelligence\tL2\t\342\235\214\t\342\235\214\nurlhaus_feed_lookup\tL2\t\342\235\214\t\342\235\214\nhtml_analysis\tL2\t\342\235\214\t\342\235\214\nattachment_static_analysis\tL2\t\342\235\214\t\342\235\214\n20. Historical Intelligence\nImplementation: FastHistoricalLookup queries DB for matching indicators\nError handling: \342\234\205 Structured logging, non-blocking (logger.warning)\nRuntime tested: \360\237\237\241 Requires PostgreSQL with historical data\nStatus: \360\237\237\241 PARTIAL\n21. Risk / Confidence / Verdict\nRisk: 0\342\200\223100, evidence-weighted, tested with BEC + SPF pass scenarios\nConfidence: 0\342\200\2231, bounded, evidence-count based\nVerdict: 5 values (BENIGN, SUSPICIOUS, MALICIOUS, INCONCLUSIVE, UNKNOWN)\nStop guard: Budget exhaustion \342\206\222 INCONCLUSIVE (never BENIGN)\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n22. Reporting\nEndpoint: GET /investigations/{id}/report \342\200\224 no longer 501\nImplementation: ReportGenerator.generate_report() with Groq\nGroq fallback: \342\234\205 Deterministic report generated when Groq unavailable\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n23. Reinvestigation\nEndpoint: POST /investigations/{id}/reinvestigate \342\200\224 fully implemented\nLineage: CaseModel.original_case_id references original\nPipeline: Full run_stage1() with persistence\nImmutability: Original case unchanged\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED (was 501 stub pre-Phase 3)\n24. Audit / Integrity\nHash chain: compute_record_hash() with GENESIS_HASH\nRecords: AuditRecordModel table\nIntegrity test: \342\234\205 Chain verified in test suite\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n25. Error Handling\nGlobal handler: @app.exception_handler(Exception) in main.py\nResponse format: {\"error\": {\"code\": \"INTERNAL_ERROR\", \"message\": \"...\", \"request_id\": \"...\"}}\nNo traceback leak: \342\234\205 Verified live\nNo credential leak: \342\234\205 Verified live\nRequest ID: \342\234\205 In response header + error body\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n26. Rate Limiting\nImplementation: In-memory dict, 10 req/min per IP\nInjection: Inside verify_api_key() \342\200\224 all auth endpoints protected\nLive test: 12 rapid requests \342\206\222 10 x 429 \342\234\205\nLimitation: Single-instance, resets on restart\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED (in-memory single-instance)\n27. CORS\nMiddleware: CORSMiddleware from env CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS\nDefault origins: http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:8501,http://localhost:5173\nNo wildcard: \342\234\205 Never uses allow_origins=[\"*\"]\nPreflight: OPTIONS returns 200 with CORS headers \342\234\205\nStatus: \342\234\205 CONFIRMED\n28. Security Findings\nFinding\tSeverity\tStatus\nAuth on all endpoints\tP0\t\342\234\205 FIXED\nCORS middleware\tP0\t\342\234\205 FIXED\nRate limiting\tP0\t\342\234\205 FIXED\nGlobal error handler\tP1\t\342\234\205 FIXED\nHistorical bare except\tP0\t\342\234\205 FIXED\nRequest ID middleware\tP2\t\342\234\205 FIXED\n.env.example template\tP2\t\342\234\205 CREATED\nReal API keys in .env\tP0\t\360\237\237\241 File is .gitignored\nAPI keys in git history\tP0\t\360\237\224\264 REQUIRES ROTATION\ndocker-compose dev creds\tP3\t\360\237\237\241 Dev-only defaults\nCurrent working tree\t\342\200\224\t\342\234\205 CLEAN\n29. Test Results\n\nPHASE 2 BASELINE:    330 total / 318 passed / 10 failed / 2 skipped\nPHASE 4 CURRENT:     358 total / 358 passed / 0 failed  / 2 skipped\nTests added in Phase 3/4: +28 Tests fixed: 10 (all stale assertions corrected to match actual runtime behavior) Regressions introduced by Phase 3: 0 (all temporarily broken tests were fixed)\n\n30. Remaining P0/P1/P2/P3\nPriority\tCount\tItems\nP0\t1\tRotate all exposed API keys from git history\nP1\t3\tSupabase PostgreSQL verification, Alembic migrations, Raw .eml storage\nP2\t3\tRBAC, distributed rate limiting, integration tests for TI providers\nP3\t2\tDocker-compose credential parameterization, disabled tool handlers\n31. Demo Readiness\n\342\234\205 BACKEND DEMO READY\nThe ASHIELDER backend can demonstrate:\n\nReal email threat investigation from raw .eml\nGenuine adaptive multi-level investigation (not a stub)\nReal ML predictions (M2 intent + M4 URL)\nEvidence-grounded risk scoring and verdict\nHash-chained audit trail\nAuthenticated API with CORS and rate limiting\nStructured error handling\n32. Production Readiness\n\360\237\224\264 BACKEND NOT PRODUCTION READY\nPrimary blockers:\n\nSupabase PostgreSQL not connected (placeholder region in URL)\nAPI keys exposed in git history (require rotation)\nNo database migrations (Alembic is a stub)\nNo raw .eml preservation\nNo RBAC\n33. Exact Next Steps\nReplace REGION in DATABASE_URL with actual Supabase region \342\206\222 verify real PostgreSQL connection\nRotate all API keys: Groq (gsk_*), VirusTotal, AbuseIPDB, IPINFO\nRun git filter-repo to remove .env from git history\nWire Alembic to SQLAlchemy models \342\206\222 create initial migration\nImplement raw .eml storage via Supabase Storage private bucket\nRun full Supabase E2E test: create \342\206\222 persist \342\206\222 restart \342\206\222 retrieve \342\206\222 verify\nAdd user model + RBAC for multi-tenant deployment\nReplace in-memory rate limiter with Redis for multi-instance\nEnable remaining TI tools (domain_intelligence, ip_intelligence, urlhaus)\nAdd integration tests for external TI providers with mocked responsesYou are now working on STAGE 2 of the ASHIELDER frontend development process.\n\nYou have already produced:\nASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md\n\nNow create a new authoritative document:\n\nASHIELDER_FRONTEND_API_INTEGRATION_SPEC.md\n\nCRITICAL RULE:\nThis document must be derived from the ACTUAL ASHIELDER BACKEND implementation and contracts, not from assumptions, mock APIs, or imagined features.\n\nYou must first inspect and understand:\n1. Existing backend API routes/endpoints\n2. HTTP methods\n3. Path parameters\n4. Query parameters\n5. Request body schemas\n6. Multipart/file upload requirements\n7. Response schemas\n8. Authentication requirements\n9. Headers required\n10. Error response structure\n11. Request-ID behavior\n12. Rate-limit behavior\n13. WebSocket/SSE support, if actually implemented\n14. Investigation lifecycle\n15. Evidence contracts\n16. Risk/confidence/verdict contracts\n17. ML outputs\n18. Tool execution outputs\n19. Historical intelligence APIs, if implemented\n20. Re-investigation behavior, if implemented\n21. Reasoning/Copilot API and its exact security boundary\n22. Reporting APIs and exact report generation behavior\n23. Database-backed case/report persistence\n24. Any endpoints that are currently stubs, incomplete, unavailable, or deferred\n\nDO NOT invent missing backend functionality.\n\nFor every frontend feature from ASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md, determine whether it is:\n\nIMPLEMENTED\nPARTIALLY IMPLEMENTED\nNOT IMPLEMENTED\nBACKEND DEPENDENT\nDEFERRED\n\nClearly mark these states.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 1 \342\200\224 BACKEND API INVENTORY\n==================================================\n\nCreate a complete endpoint inventory.\n\nFor every endpoint provide:\n\n- Endpoint name\n- HTTP method\n- Path\n- Purpose\n- Authentication requirement\n- Required headers\n- Request parameters\n- Request body schema\n- Multipart requirements\n- Response schema\n- Error schema\n- Expected status codes\n- Rate-limit behavior\n- Timeout considerations\n- Frontend consumer\n- Current backend implementation status\n\nUse the REAL endpoint names and REAL field names.\n\nNever rename backend fields simply to make them prettier.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 2 \342\200\224 FRONTEND FEATURE \342\206\222 API MAPPING\n==================================================\n\nMap the Stage 1 product features to backend capabilities.\n\nCreate a matrix:\n\nFrontend Feature\n\342\206\222 Required API\n\342\206\222 Backend Status\n\342\206\222 Data Required\n\342\206\222 Missing Dependency\n\342\206\222 Frontend Fallback\n\nInclude at minimum:\n\n- Authentication\n- Case Inbox\n- Case filtering\n- Case search\n- Case workspace\n- Email/EML ingestion\n- Investigation status\n- Investigation journey\n- Risk\n- Confidence\n- Verdict\n- Key findings\n- Evidence Explorer\n- Evidence detail\n- Header Forensics\n- Authentication results\n- Received hop chain\n- URL analysis\n- Domain intelligence\n- IP intelligence\n- GeoLocation\n- Attachments\n- Historical intelligence\n- Re-investigation\n- Forensic report\n- AI Copilot\n- Error states\n- Inconclusive cases\n- Audit information\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 3 \342\200\224 EXACT TYPESCRIPT CONTRACTS\n==================================================\n\nGenerate frontend TypeScript interfaces/types directly from the backend contracts.\n\nExamples:\n\nCase\nInvestigation\nEvidence\nEvidenceType\nVerdict\nRisk\nConfidence\nHeaderEvidence\nAuthenticationEvidence\nURLIntel\nIPIntel\nGeoLocation\nAttachmentEvidence\nHistoricalMatch\nInvestigationStep\nToolExecution\nReasoningResult\nForensicReport\nApiError\n\nDo not create fields that do not exist in backend contracts.\n\nFor uncertain fields, mark them explicitly rather than guessing.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 4 \342\200\224 API CLIENT CONTRACT\n==================================================\n\nDefine exactly how the frontend should communicate with FastAPI.\n\nSpecify:\n\n- Base URL configuration\n- Environment variables\n- HTTP client strategy\n- Authentication injection\n- X-Request-ID handling\n- Timeout handling\n- Retry policy\n- 401 handling\n- 403 handling\n- 404 handling\n- 413 handling\n- 422 handling\n- 429 handling\n- 500 handling\n- network failure handling\n\nThe frontend must NEVER expose backend API keys, provider secrets, Groq keys, threat-intelligence keys, database credentials, or other server secrets.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 5 \342\200\224 AUTHENTICATION FLOW\n==================================================\n\nDocument the ACTUAL authentication model.\n\nShow:\n\nFrontend\n\342\206\222 Authentication\n\342\206\222 token/API-key/session\n\342\206\222 FastAPI\n\342\206\222 protected endpoint\n\nClearly distinguish:\n- implemented authentication\n- planned authentication\n- Firebase authentication, if actually present\n- backend API-key authentication, if actually present\n- future enterprise SSO/RBAC\n\nDo NOT present future RBAC/SSO as currently implemented.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 6 \342\200\224 CASE LIFECYCLE\n==================================================\n\nDescribe the actual lifecycle:\n\nUPLOAD\n\342\206\222 INGESTION\n\342\206\222 ANALYSIS\n\342\206\222 INVESTIGATION\n\342\206\222 RISK EVALUATION\n\342\206\222 VERDICT\n\342\206\222 REPORT\n\nUse actual backend states.\n\nFor every state explain:\n\n- API response\n- frontend visual state\n- polling/WebSocket requirement\n- terminal/non-terminal\n- retry behavior\n- failure behavior\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 7 \342\200\224 INVESTIGATION JOURNEY MAPPING\n==================================================\n\nMap every backend investigation stage to frontend journey visualization.\n\nFor each stage define:\n\nStage\nStatus\nTimestamp\nDuration\nEvidence produced\nTool used\nError/degraded state\nFrontend representation\n\nThe frontend must distinguish:\n\nSUCCESS\nDEGRADED\nFAILED\nPENDING\nSKIPPED\n\nDo not create a stage unless it actually exists in backend behavior.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 8 \342\200\224 EVIDENCE MODEL\n==================================================\n\nTreat Evidence as the central frontend data model.\n\nDocument:\n\n- Evidence ID\n- Evidence type\n- Source\n- Producer\n- Provenance\n- Value\n- Confidence/reliability if available\n- Corroboration if available\n- Timestamp\n- Hash/integrity fields if available\n\nExplain how the frontend links:\n\nFinding\n\342\206\222 Evidence ID\n\342\206\222 Evidence detail\n\342\206\222 Raw source\n\nPreserve the ASHIELDER rule:\n\nEVIDENCE FIRST.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 9 \342\200\224 RISK / CONFIDENCE / VERDICT\n==================================================\n\nDocument the exact backend meaning and field structure for:\n\nRisk\nConfidence\nVerdict\n\nThe UI must NEVER merge Risk and Confidence into a single metric.\n\nExplicitly handle:\n\nBENIGN\nSUSPICIOUS\nMALICIOUS\nINCONCLUSIVE\n\nIf backend semantics differ from Stage 1 mock examples, backend behavior is authoritative.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 10 \342\200\224 AI COPILOT INTEGRATION\n==================================================\n\nInspect the implemented reasoning layer.\n\nDocument exactly:\n\n- endpoint\n- request\n- response\n- evidence snapshot\n- reasoning fields\n- validation\n- citations\n- unavailable state\n- timeout\n- failure behavior\n- audit behavior\n\nExplicitly enforce:\n\nAI CANNOT:\n- modify verdict\n- modify risk\n- modify confidence\n- execute arbitrary tools\n- mutate investigation state\n\nAI IS:\n- advisory\n- evidence-grounded\n- bounded\n- auditable\n\nDo not invent chat streaming if backend does not support it.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 11 \342\200\224 REPORTING\n==================================================\n\nMap frontend report screens to actual backend report functionality.\n\nIdentify:\n\n- available report endpoint\n- report schema\n- generated sections\n- PDF availability\n- report status\n- hash/integrity information\n- missing report functionality\n\nClearly distinguish:\nIMPLEMENTED\nINLINE ONLY\nDEFERRED\nNOT IMPLEMENTED\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 12 \342\200\224 REAL ERROR/DEGRADED STATES\n==================================================\n\nCreate frontend behavior for actual backend failures.\n\nInclude:\n\n- malformed EML\n- validation failure\n- authentication failure\n- authorization failure\n- rate limiting\n- database failure\n- tool timeout\n- external API failure\n- investigation failure\n- reasoning unavailable\n- missing evidence\n- inconclusive result\n- network disconnect\n\nFor each:\n\nBackend signal\n\342\206\222 UI state\n\342\206\222 User message\n\342\206\222 Recovery action\n\342\206\222 Retry allowed?\n\342\206\222 Risk impact\n\342\206\222 Confidence impact\n\nImportant:\n\nMISSING EVIDENCE \342\211\240 SAFE.\n\nTool failure must never automatically become BENIGN.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 13 \342\200\224 LOADING / POLLING / REAL-TIME\n==================================================\n\nDetermine whether the backend actually supports:\n\n- polling\n- WebSockets\n- SSE\n- synchronous responses\n\nDo not assume real-time support.\n\nIf only polling is available, define polling strategy.\n\nIf nothing exists, clearly state that frontend should use a fallback strategy.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 14 \342\200\224 SECURITY BOUNDARY\n==================================================\n\nDocument the frontend security rules.\n\nNever:\n\n- expose secrets\n- execute raw email HTML unsafely\n- automatically navigate to extracted URLs\n- render active malware\n- execute attachment content\n- trust AI-generated text as authoritative\n- allow frontend-only verdict mutation\n\nAll URLs should remain defanged unless an explicit backend-supported safe workflow exists.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 15 \342\200\224 BACKEND GAPS\n==================================================\n\nCreate a dedicated section:\n\nASHIELDER_FRONTEND_BACKEND_GAPS\n\nList everything Stage 1 expects but backend does not currently provide.\n\nFor each gap include:\n\n- Feature\n- Missing API/data\n- Why frontend needs it\n- Priority\n- Recommended backend contract\n- Whether it is required for SIH MVP\n\nDO NOT implement or fabricate these APIs.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 16 \342\200\224 MVP BOUNDARY\n==================================================\n\nDefine a realistic frontend MVP.\n\nSeparate:\n\nMUST HAVE\nSHOULD HAVE\nCAN WAIT\nNOT BACKEND-SUPPORTED\n\nThe MVP should focus on actual working backend capabilities instead of visually impressive mock functionality.\n\n==================================================\nSECTION 17 \342\200\224 FINAL API CONTRACT TABLE\n==================================================\n\nEnd the document with one consolidated table:\n\nFeature\nEndpoint\nMethod\nAuth\nRequest\nResponse\nStatus\nFrontend Component\nBackend Availability\n\nThis table becomes the single source of truth for frontend implementation.\n\n==================================================\nFINAL RULES\n==================================================\n\n1. Backend implementation is the source of truth.\n2. Stage 1 defines UX/product intent.\n3. Never invent an API.\n4. Never invent response fields.\n5. Never present future functionality as implemented.\n6. Preserve exact backend terminology.\n7. Clearly mark unavailable functionality.\n8. Keep Evidence IDs and provenance first-class.\n9. Keep Risk and Confidence separate.\n10. Keep AI advisory and non-authoritative.\n11. Design for graceful degraded/inconclusive states.\n12. Do not redesign the backend.\n13. Do not write frontend code yet.\n14. This document is a CONTRACT SPECIFICATION, not an implementation plan.\n\nOUTPUT:\nCreate the complete file:\n\nASHIELDER_FRONTEND_API_INTEGRATION_SPEC.md\n\nMake it detailed enough that a frontend engineer can implement the entire client without needing to guess backend behavior."
  }
}
